Anchor's errors are `"<code>: <message>"`, and the codes are protocol,
not prose — matching on them is the stable thing to do. An unrecognised
code is `Internal` rather than a guess: a newer daemon saying something
this build does not understand is exactly when not to improvise.rust-analyzer cargo teleportd 0.1.0 anchor_bridge/BridgedSession#structBridgedSessionpub struct BridgedSessionWhat teleport knows about an anchor-hosted session, for its API views.

The four fields teleport serves that anchor has no equivalent for —
`title`, `claude_resume_id`, `last_bell_ms`, `idle_since_ms` — are not
here. They are derived from the output stream, which teleport sees
anyway when it attaches, and they stay teleport's to compute: each is a
policy about what a UI shows, and a second anchor client would want
different ones (anchor `docs/01-scope.md`).rust-analyzer cargo teleportd 0.1.0 anchor_bridge/BridgedSession#session.fieldsessionpub session: AnchorSessionThe session as anchor reports it.rust-analyzer cargo teleportd 0.1.0 anchor_bridge/impl#[BridgedSession]new().methodnewpub fn new(session: AnchorSession) -> SelfWraps an anchor session.rust-analyzer cargo teleportd 0.1.0 anchor_bridge/impl#[BridgedSession]kind().kindpub fn kind(&self) -> StringTeleport's `kind`, from the label it set at create.

Defaults to `"shell"` for a session anchor holds that teleport did
not create — another client's session is still visible, and a
