disk, and a child that is *still emitting* while the client catches up.

Three phases, in order. The backlog, so there is something to walk. Then a
blocking `read` on the pty, which is the whole trick -- it lets the test
decide, rather than guess, when live output starts, so the control
subscriber below is provably registered for all of it. Then `BURST`, and
finally the trickle that keeps the session producing for the rest of the
walk.

`read` rather than `head -n 1`: it is a shell builtin that consumes one
byte at a time from a non-seekable fd, so it cannot swallow output the
test has not accounted for.rust-analyzer cargo teleportd 0.1.0 attaching_far_behind_a_producing_session_reaches_live().attaching_far_behind_a_producing_session_reaches_liveasync fn attaching_far_behind_a_producing_session_reaches_live()teleportd::session::{SessionManager, MAX_QUEUE_BYTES}local tests/session_catchup.rs 0local tests/session_catchup.rs 1local tests/session_catchup.rs 2local tests/session_catchup.rs 3local tests/session_catchup.rs 4local tests/session_catchup.rs 5local tests/session_catchup.rs 6local tests/session_catchup.rs 7registered_firstlet registered_first: Subscriptionlocal tests/session_catchup.rs 8local tests/session_catchup.rs 9local tests/session_catchup.rs 10ready_next_offsetlet ready_next_offset: u64local tests/session_catchup.rs 11local tests/session_catchup.rs 12local tests/session_catchup.rs 13local tests/session_catchup.rs 14let mut end: u64local tests/session_catchup.rs 15local tests/session_catchup.rs 16let i: i32local tests/session_catchup.rs 17local tests/session_catchup.rs 18old_ordering_diedlet old_ordering_died: Result<(), Elapsed>local tests/session_catchup.rs 19local tests/session_catchup.rs 20rust-analyzer cargo core https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/library/core cmp/impls/impl#[u32][`PartialOrd<Self>`]gt().rust-analyzer cargo teleportd 0.1.0 BURST.tests/session_replay.rslocal tests/session_replay.rs support/local tests/session_replay.rs RECV_TIMEOUT.local tests/session_replay.rs sessions_root().rust-analyzer cargo teleportd 0.1.0 spawn_emitting().spawn_emittingfn spawn_emitting(manager: &SessionManager, bytes: usize) -> std::sync::Arc<Session>A session that emits exactly `bytes` bytes of `yes` output as fast as it
can, then blocks forever reading its own stdin. `stty raw -echo` keeps
the tty line discipline from injecting anything of its own, so the log is
the child's output and nothing else.

Every fixture here reaches this output through `attach`, never a bare
`subscribe()`: the child is already producing by the time `create`
returns, and only `attach` accounts for the bytes that landed before the
caller registered.

The trailing `cat` reads nothing real -- nothing ever writes to this
pty's input side -- it exists only to hold the slave fd open past the
last byte of output, so these fixtures' exact-byte-count assertions never
race the child's own exit: the session ends when the test calls
`terminate()`, on the test's schedule, not the child's. Every caller
terminates the session when it's done.rust-analyzer cargo teleportd 0.1.0 spawn_emitting_forever().spawn_emitting_foreverfn spawn_emitting_forever(manager: &SessionManager) -> std::sync::Arc<Session>A session that emits 64 KiB bursts, forever, at roughly 1 MB/s -- the
sustained rate the M2 gate is stated in. Used where a fixture needs live
output to still be flowing *after* it has inspected the log; a bounded
emitter finishes long before the assertions run.rust-analyzer cargo teleportd 0.1.0 disconnect_between_chunks_and_reconnect_has_no_gap_or_duplicate().disconnect_between_chunks_and_reconnect_has_no_gap_or_duplicateasync fn disconnect_between_chunks_and_reconnect_has_no_gap_or_duplicate()**The M3 gate.** Attach at 0, take live chunks, disconnect exactly
between two of them, let output keep flowing with zero subscribers,
reconnect at the recorded offset, and check byte-for-byte that
replay + live equals the log -- no gap, no duplicate.

Was `target_os = "linux"`-gated: it failed on `macos-latest` with "first
subscriber disconnected early" during the initial live-drain loop, which
was
[N5](../../docs/15-open-questions.md#n5--macos-pty-reads-average-14-bytes-starving-the-queue-bounds-count-half)
-- the 256-chunk half of the queue bound holding only ~3.5 KiB on macOS,
where a pty read averages 14 bytes. Un-gated with the fix that made the
bound a byte budget.

It was also un-gated once *before* that fix, on 20/20 local passes, and
failed first try on the runner. Worth keeping in mind if this ever goes
red again: this file's suite takes 20.2 s on `macos-latest` and 7.6 s on
an M-series Mac, and a subscriber that never stalls never trips a queue
bound, so a green local run says very little here. Whatever the next
theory is, get it green on the runner before believing it.rust-analyzer cargo teleportd 0.1.0 TOTAL.TOTALconst TOTAL: usizerust-analyzer cargo teleportd 0.1.0 repeated_attach_against_a_concurrent_writer_never_gaps().repeated_attach_against_a_concurrent_writer_never_gapsasync fn repeated_attach_against_a_concurrent_writer_never_gaps()The same boundary, hammered: re-attach over and over against a writer
