another's list and uncounted by its `max_sessions`.rust-analyzer cargo teleportd 0.1.0 support/NEXT.NEXTstatic NEXT: std::sync::atomic::AtomicU64rust-analyzer cargo teleportd 0.1.0 support/SHARED.SHAREDstatic SHARED: std::sync::OnceLock<Option<Anchord>>The one `anchord` this test binary shares.

One per binary, not one per fixture: a daemon per fixture meant dozens
of processes with four threads per live session, and creates began
failing under that load — a harness that cannot hold still is no way to
judge a product. Fixtures stay isolated by owning their sessions under a
unique `anchor_owner`, which is the same mechanism that keeps two real
tools sharing one daemon out of each other's lists.

Never dropped: a `static` lives for the process, so the daemon outlives
every fixture in the binary and its directory is cleaned up by the OS.rust-analyzer cargo teleportd 0.1.0 support/wipe_shared_daemon().wipe_shared_daemonasync fn wipe_shared_daemon(socket: &Path)Wipes every session out of the shared daemon, once per run.

Reusing a daemon across runs stops it leaking *processes*, but each run
leaves its sessions behind — most of them still running, since fixtures
rarely terminate what they spawn. Those accumulate into exactly the
resource pressure the reuse was meant to end. The daemon belongs
entirely to this test binary, so starting each run from empty is both
safe and what isolation actually requires.rust-analyzer cargo teleportd 0.1.0 support/WIPED.WIPEDstatic WIPED: tokio::sync::OnceCell<()>shared_anchord_socketpub(crate) fn shared_anchord_socket() -> Option<PathBuf>The shared daemon's socket, or `None` when not running on anchor.rust-analyzer cargo teleportd 0.1.0 support/Anchord#pub(crate) struct AnchordAn `anchord` the suite drives, so teleport's own tests can run against
